+A single fermion field changes the total particle number by one. By contrast, a charge-density-wave (CDW) operator removes a left-moving fermion and creates a right-moving fermion, while a superconducting (SC) pair-annihilation operator removes one fermion of each chirality. Both are composite operators formed by multiplying two fields. This composition introduces a question that does not arise for single fields: \emph{what happens to the intermediate states between the first and second operations when calculations are restricted to a specific energy-budget subspace?}
+
+In the full, unrestricted finite fermionic Fock space, we multiply the operators in their defined order. Imposing a strict energy budget changes the situation. Multiplying two \emph{individually projected} fields forces the system to pass entirely through a chosen intermediate budget subspace. In contrast, projecting the \emph{full product} allows intermediate states to exceed the budget, provided they return to an allowed state before the final projection. These temporary excursions out of the intermediate budget produce an explicit mathematical remainder. 
+
+This section isolates and tracks this remainder. Working out this finite-lattice product provides a setting to clarify chirality, charge labels, Fourier phases, and fermionic ordering signs. These details must be established before defining formal vertex operators in Section~\ref{sec:ch20-formal-vertices}. 
